# Code Reuse First

## Context
- When creating new services, logic, tools, or functions
- When implementing new features with similar functionality to existing ones
- When planning architectural changes or extensions

## Requirements
- Always scan the codebase for similar existing implementations before creating new code
- Prefer extending or modifying existing utilities over creating new ones
- Abstract commonly used patterns into reusable utilities or helpers
- Document reused components and their intended use cases
- Maintain a centralized location for common utilities and services
- Consider creating a shared library for cross-project code reuse

## Examples
<example>
✅ Before creating a new date formatting function, check if `utils/date.js` already has similar functionality:
```js
// GOOD: Reusing existing utility
import { formatDate } from '../utils/date';
const formattedDate = formatDate(timestamp, 'YYYY-MM-DD');
```
</example>

<example type="invalid">
❌ Creating duplicate functionality:
```js
// BAD: Reinventing the wheel
function formatMyDate(timestamp) {
  const date = new Date(timestamp);
  return `${date.getFullYear()}-${(date.getMonth() + 1).toString().padStart(2, '0')}-${date.getDate().toString().padStart(2, '0')}`;
}
```
</example>

<example>
✅ When similar functionality exists but doesn't quite fit your needs, extend it:
```js
// GOOD: Extending existing functionality
export function enhancedAPIClient(config) {
  const baseClient = createAPIClient(config);
  
  return {
    ...baseClient,
    // Add new methods while preserving original functionality
    newFeature: async () => {
      // Implementation using baseClient
    }
  };
}
```
</example>

<example type="invalid">
❌ Creating a completely new implementation:
```js
// BAD: Creating parallel implementation
export function myCustomAPIClient(config) {
  // Reimplementing much of the same logic as createAPIClient
  // with slight variations
}
```
</example>

## Critical Rules
  - Always search for keywords in the codebase related to your task before implementation
  - Prioritize maintainability through code reuse over short-term convenience
  - If creating new code, ensure it's generalized enough to be reusable by others
  - Regularly refactor common patterns into shared utilities
  - Document reused components thoroughly to encourage further reuse